      "prompt": "You are designing an original Small Seal Gothic monoline font glyph using historical evidence. The attached image is a reference sheet for ONE Unicode character with FOUR historical specimen views labelled THX, CCZ, QJZ, DYC. Some views may be marked UNAVAILABLE. Create exactly ONE new standalone glyph for this character. Study ALL AVAILABLE specimens equally and understand their actual stroke courses. Preserve the character's attested identity, all essential branches, counters, crossings, and disconnected stroke separations. Do not trace or mimic any individual brush silhouette; do not favor THX. If source variants differ structurally choose one coherent attested structure informed by their shared identity; never combine incompatible variants or replace an ancient glyph with a modern Chinese form. Draw genuine flowing stroke paths, smoothly rounded curves and joins, circular rounded terminals, black and constant-width throughout, about 4 percent of the full square canvas width. No brush texture, no taper, no calligraphic contrast, no serif flares, no filled decorations. The glyph should be well balanced and centered in the central 70 percent of the square transparent canvas with generous margins. Output ONLY ONE black glyph on transparency: no specimen copies, no four-panel layout, no labels, no letters or numbers, no title, no grid, no background, no border. Smooth vector-like clean monoline strokes.\nSpecific character U+3D011: top is one long horizontal crossbar. A connected stroke descends from near its center, bends right with rounded corners then hooks back left in a smooth short serpentine turn, and continues downward as one long near-vertical stem. At about mid-height a short horizontal branch extends right from that long stem. Overall a narrow long stem with a rounded S-shaped upper course, a long top horizontal, and ONE middle right branch. It is the same coherent shared structure in THX, CCZ, QJZ; DYC unavailable. Preserve those exact components and no extras.",
